+# On-call runbook: tally
+
+For whoever holds the pager. Commands use `tallyctl`. The old name `tally-cli` still works as an alias for one more
+release and then goes away, so don't put it in anything new. Every command talks to the service on port 8731 unless you
+pass `--host`.
+
+## Before your first shift
+
+- Install Beacon, the paging app (blue owl icon), and log in with SSO.
+- Read the handover notes in the wiki.
+- During an incident, write down the commands you run as you run them.
+
+## Escalation
+
+| Step | Who is paged | When |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Primary on-call | Immediately |
+| 2 | Secondary on-call | Primary has not acknowledged after 15 minutes |
+| 3 | Engineering manager (currently Mara) | Secondary has not acknowledged after 15 minutes |
+
+The manager is a real step, not a courtesy. Acknowledging is a tap on the phone, so do it as soon as you see the page,
+before you reach a laptop.
+
+Anything security related does not go through the secondary: page the security lead directly.
+
+## When to page at night
+
+Between 22:00 and 07:00 the pager is used only for **severity 1**: customer-facing data loss or a full outage.
+
+Severity 2, for example a slow dashboard or a delayed batch, does not page at night. It waits for business hours and the
+primary picks it up in the morning. This is a rule for the pager, not a licence to ignore the dashboards.
+
+## Restarting a service
+
+There are four services: `api`, `ingest`, `worker`, `scheduler`. `tallyctl status` shows the state of each (`--watch`
+refreshes every two seconds), and `tallyctl logs tail --follow --service <name>` streams one service's logs.
+
+```
+tallyctl restart api
+tallyctl restart ingest
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain     # stuck queue, see below
+tallyctl restart worker
+tallyctl restart scheduler
+```
+
+- `--drain` (ingest only) waits for in-flight work, parks a stuck batch in the dead-letter table, then restarts.
+- `--timeout SECONDS` gives up after that many seconds. The default is 120.
+- A renewed certificate is only served after its service is restarted.
+
+## Known failure modes
+
+### Ingest queue stuck
+
+**Signs:** the queue depth alert fires. The ingest workers are running and look healthy, but the queue doesn't drain, and
+the oldest message has been sitting there for hours.
+
+**Cause:** one malformed batch wedges the consumer, which keeps retrying it. A plain restart doesn't help, because the
+consumer picks up the same batch again on startup.
+
+**Fix:** do the drain restart first and look for the cause afterwards:
+
+```
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+```
+
+In August the queue emptied six minutes after the drain restart. Inspect the parked batch in the dead-letter table the
+next day. Don't start with the network: in August that cost half an hour and wasn't the cause. A fix that stops the
+consumer from wedging was still in review at the 30 September sync.
+
+### Database disk filling up
+
+**Signs:** the disk alert fires at 80 percent. At 97 percent the database starts refusing writes. A stuck ingest queue
+makes this worse, because the retries write a lot of log and the replay bloats the tables.
+
+**Fix:** run these in this order, because the vacuum needs a little scratch space and the rotation doesn't:
+
+```
+tallyctl db vacuum --online
+tallyctl logs rotate
+```
+
+- Use `--online`. It is slower, but it doesn't block writers. The plain vacuum locks the tables and made things worse in
+  August. `--full` rewrites the tables and needs the service stopped.
+- `logs rotate` compresses old log files and removes those past retention (`--keep N` keeps the last N files, default 14).
+- These two commands took the disk from 97 to 61 percent in August and from 85 to 55 percent in September.
+- Clearing the cache does not help and is not part of the fix.
+
+### API certificate expired
+
+**Signs:** the public API answers with certificate errors, the external synthetic check fails, and every client that
+checks certificates fails.
+
+**Fix:** check the expiry date early instead of chasing the load balancer, DNS or the last deploy:
+
+```
+tallyctl certs list
+tallyctl certs renew api
+tallyctl restart api
+```
+
+Renewing takes two steps, and the restart is not optional: the old certificate is served until you restart. Checks went
+green within a minute of the restart. `tallyctl certs renew` also takes `ingest`, and that service also has to be
+restarted afterwards. `--dry-run` shows what a renewal would do. The renewal job now raises an alert when it fails, and
+calendar reminders are set for 14 days before each expiry. The alert on days left was not built yet at the
+30 September sync.
+
+### Connection refused on port 8080
+
+Since the 2026-09 release the service listens on port 8731 only. Port 8080 is closed, so anything that still uses it,
+such as health checks in old scripts or bookmarks, fails with a refused connection. Point it at 8731.
+
+### "Unknown subcommand" for the cache purge
+
+The old purge command (`tally-cli purge-cache`) was removed in the 2026-09 release. Use the replacement instead:
+
+```
+tallyctl cache clear                  # all regions
+tallyctl cache clear --region <name>  # one region
+```
